## 1. The parties and acceptance

This agreement is between the licensor, Tijn van der Zant, and you.
"You" is the individual or organisation that uses CTOC. If you use CTOC
for or on behalf of a company or another organisation, you accept this
agreement for that organisation too.

Using CTOC means accepting this agreement. If you do not accept it, you
may not use CTOC.

Every license in this agreement is granted on condition that you keep
its terms. They are binding obligations and conditions of each license.

Questions about this agreement go to tijn@theaiguys.com.

## 2. Definitions

CTOC is the software CTO Chief (CTOC) that the licensor makes available
with this agreement: its source code, documentation, agents and skills,
every part of it, and every copy of it.

Use is anything you do with CTOC, including installing, running,
copying, studying and changing it.

Your company is any company, partnership or other legal entity for or
on behalf of which you use CTOC, together with its affiliates.
Affiliates of an organisation are the organisations it controls, the
organisations that control it, and the organisations under common
control with it. Control is owning substantially all the assets of an
organisation, or having the power to direct its management and policies
by vote, by contract or in any other way, directly or indirectly.

A consumer is a natural person who acts for purposes outside their
trade, business, craft or profession.

Business use is:

(a) any use by or on behalf of a company, partnership or other legal
    entity; and

(b) any use by an individual in the course of paid work, a trade, a
    business or a profession.

Non-commercial use is not business use. It is:

(c) use by a charitable organisation, an educational institution, a
    public research organisation, a public safety or health
    organisation, an environmental protection organisation or a
    government institution, for its non-commercial purposes,
    regardless of its source of funding, and the work of an
    individual on that organisation's behalf; and

(d) personal use: use by an individual outside paid work, a trade, a
    business or a profession, and not for or on behalf of a company,
    partnership or other legal entity, such as study, a hobby or an
    unpaid project of their own.

A use that falls under (c) is not business use, even where (a) or (b)
also describes it.

(e) Use for a commercial product or a paid service is business use.
    That is a product or service that is sold, licensed or provided for
    payment. It is business use also when an organisation listed in (c),
    or an individual under (d), makes the product or provides the
    service. Paragraph (e) overrides (c) and (d).

    Payment means money or other value that you, or the organisation
    you act for, receive in exchange for that product or service. Only
    these are not payment under this paragraph: the wages of an
    individual; tuition that an educational institution listed in (c)
    receives for its teaching; fees that a public safety or health
    organisation listed in (c) receives for its care; fees that a
    government institution listed in (c) charges for its public tasks;
    and grants, donations and public funding. Any other product or
    service that an organisation listed in (c) sells, licenses or
    provides for payment is business use, also when the money it brings
    in supports a non-commercial purpose.

Evaluation is use to find out whether CTOC suits a particular use.

## 3. Free use

The licensor grants you a non-exclusive, non-transferable, worldwide
license, free of charge, to use CTOC for personal use and for
non-commercial use. You may install and run CTOC, make the copies that
this use needs, and change CTOC and use the changed copy for that same
use. Changed copies stay private (section 6).

## 4. Evaluation

Free of charge, and on the same conditions as section 3, apart from its
limit to personal and non-commercial use, the licensor lets any person
or company use CTOC for evaluation, business evaluation included, for
less than 32 consecutive calendar days, counted from the first day of
that evaluation.

The period runs once per company together with its affiliates, or once
per individual who acts for no company. It does not start again for
another person or for another use. If you use CTOC for your company,
the period is your company's, and it covers everyone who uses CTOC for
your company.

Business use that goes on after the period needs a commercial license
(section 5).

## 5. Business use needs a commercial license

Any business use other than evaluation under section 4 needs a paid
commercial license from the licensor. This agreement gives no right to
business use beyond section 4.

Ask for a commercial license at tijn@theaiguys.com. A commercial
license is a separate agreement with the licensor. It is made in
writing, or by electronic means that keep a durable record of it, and it
sets its own terms.

## 6. What you may not do

Unless a commercial license with the licensor expressly allows it, you
may not:

(a) distribute, publish, sell, rent, lend or otherwise make CTOC or any
    part of it available to anyone else, except as section 7 states;

(b) sublicense, assign or transfer any right under this agreement;

(c) distribute or make available a changed copy of CTOC, or any work
    based on CTOC;

(d) provide a product or service that competes with CTOC, or use CTOC
    to provide one. A product competes with CTOC even if it offers the
    same functionality through a different kind of interface, for a
    different technical platform or in a different programming
    language. It competes even if it is provided free of charge. A
    product marketed as a practical substitute for CTOC competes with
    it;

(e) remove, change or hide the copyright notice, this agreement, or any
    copyright, license or attribution notice in CTOC.

## 7. Viewing and forking on GitHub

Viewing and forking CTOC's repository within GitHub, under GitHub's
terms of service, does not breach this agreement. This gives you no
right outside GitHub beyond what this agreement grants. A fork that
holds changes to CTOC is a changed copy under section 6(c).

## 8. Rights the law gives you

This agreement limits none of the rights that a lawful user of CTOC has
under European Union and Dutch law on computer programs. These include
loading and running the program and correcting its errors; making a
back-up copy; observing, studying and testing how CTOC works; and
decompiling it for interoperability (Directive 2009/24/EC, articles 5
and 6; Dutch Copyright Act, articles 45j to 45m). Nor does it limit any
other right the law gives you that a contract cannot take away.

## 9. No warranty

As far as the law allows, CTOC is provided as it is, without any
warranty or condition of any kind, express or implied.

## 10. Liability

Toward anyone who uses CTOC for business use, business evaluation
included, and toward every other user who is not a consumer, the
licensor's liability for any damage arising out of this agreement or out
of the use of CTOC is excluded as far as the law allows. A commercial
license may set different terms.

Nothing in this agreement excludes or limits:

- liability for intent or deliberate recklessness;
- product liability toward natural persons for death, personal injury,
  or damage to property or data not used for professional purposes;
- a consumer's statutory rights;
- any other liability that the law does not allow to be excluded or
  limited.

## 11. Governing law and courts

Dutch law governs this agreement. If you are a consumer, this choice
does not take away the protection of the mandatory provisions of the law
of the country where you have your habitual residence.

The competent court in the Netherlands has jurisdiction over any
dispute arising out of this agreement. This is without prejudice to the
courts that a consumer may use, or must be sued in, under European Union
law.

## 12. No other rights

The licensor keeps every right that this agreement does not expressly
grant. This agreement implies no other license, and it does not stop the
licensor from granting licenses to anyone else.
